WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.877
A recovery time objective, or RTO, is a target for how quickly a system or process should be restored after disruption.

00:00:09.877 --> 00:00:17.560
A recovery point objective, or RPO, describes how much data or configuration change can be lost.

00:00:17.560 --> 00:00:23.047
Maximum tolerable downtime marks the point beyond which impact becomes unacceptable.

00:00:23.047 --> 00:00:25.901
These objectives need OT interpretation.

00:00:25.901 --> 00:00:34.461
Restoring an HMI server in two hours does not mean the process can safely restart in two hours.

00:00:34.461 --> 00:00:44.339
Equipment may require inspection, utilities may need stabilization, logic may need validation, and upstream or downstream operations may not be ready.

00:00:44.339 --> 00:00:47.631
Safety-first decision-making sets boundaries around speed.

00:00:47.631 --> 00:00:57.069
A process should remain shut down or degraded when alarms, interlocks, field states, communications, or operator visibility cannot be trusted.

00:00:57.069 --> 00:01:01.240
Business pressure does not change those physical conditions.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:04.093
Prioritize restoration by process dependency.

00:01:04.093 --> 00:01:12.215
Foundational utilities, networks, time, identity, controllers, and operator visibility may need to recover before historians or reporting.

00:01:12.215 --> 00:01:20.336
The exact sequence varies by facility and should be engineered rather than copied from a generic checklist.

00:01:20.336 --> 00:01:27.580
Objectives should include people, spares, vendor response, and startup duration, not just backup restore time.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:34.384
Define who can authorize degraded operation, safe shutdown, technical recovery, and return to production.

00:01:34.384 --> 00:01:39.871
Escalation paths should resolve conflicts between safety, operations, and business needs.

00:01:39.871 --> 00:01:41.847
Make objectives testable.

00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:49.090
Exercises should measure actual restore and restart times, data loss, validation effort, and unmet prerequisites.

00:01:49.090 --> 00:01:58.529
If results do not meet the target, improve resources or revise the objective transparently rather than preserving an unrealistic promise.
